PHASE 2 - A USABLE ASSISTANT: CLARIFY AND SCREENSHOTS
=====================================================
Written 2026-08-16 on the M3 (96 GB), before the local models were available on
this machine, and verified on the M4 Air on 2026-08-21 against the real helpers
and the real model. What that verification proved, and the two bugs it found, are
under "Verified on the M4 Air"; what is still outstanding is under "Still to
verify".


What is new
-----------
CLARIFY (alt-cmd-C)
  A second transcript command over a shorter window, 45 s by default. Its prompt
  asks for the words rather than the concepts: most likely verbatim wording
  first, marked where it was repaired, then one or two sentences of meaning, and
  the plausible readings where a passage is genuinely ambiguous. It assumes
  misrecognition and a strong accent, which is what EXPLAIN does not.

SCREENSHOT (alt-cmd-S)
  Archival only. bin/screen_capture writes a PNG, the assistant writes a sidecar
  JSON beside it, and nothing else happens: no OCR, no model, no network, no
  visible UI. The Hammerspoon binding is silent on success precisely so that
  nothing appears in a shared screen; a failure is still shown.

INTERPRET_SCREEN (ctrl-alt-cmd-S)
  The identical archival save, followed by a request to a local vision model
  with the image and the last 120 s of transcript. The answer is streamed into
  the assistant's own terminal, never onto the screen. If the save works and the
  interpretation fails, the image is still archived: the save happens first and
  the model request runs on a separate thread.


Archive layout
--------------
  var/meetings/YYYY/MM/YYYY-MM-DD_HH.MM/screenshots/YYYY-MM-DD_HH.MM.SS.mmm.png
                                                   /YYYY-MM-DD_HH.MM.SS.mmm.json

The session directory is named from the time the assistant started, so one run
collects its images in one place; the image name is the capture time to the
millisecond, so a burst cannot collide and any image can be aligned with a
transcript timestamp. Phase 3 can drop metadata.json and transcript.jsonl into
the same session directory without moving anything. RVW_ARCHIVE_DIR moves the
root; var/ is git ignored.

The sidecar JSON records what the helper saw and what only the assistant knows:

  {"application": "zoom.us", "bundle_id": "us.zoom.xos", "window_title": "Zoom
   Meeting", "window_id": 5142, "target": "window", "width": 3024, "height":
   1890, "bytes": 1442310, "captured_epoch": 1786552867.123, "captured_local":
   "2026-08-15T23:41:07"}

That metadata is nearly free at capture time and expensive to reconstruct later,
which is the whole reason for writing it now.


Design decisions
----------------
Screen capture is its own Swift helper, built by the same helper/build.sh with
its own embedded Info.plist and its own ad hoc signature (ai.rvw.screen_capture),
so the screen recording permission and any screen capture failure stay isolated
from audio.

The helper captures the frontmost application's largest on screen window using
ScreenCaptureKit and SCScreenshotManager. Whole display capture exists as
--target display (RVW_SCREENSHOT_TARGET=display) but is never chosen
automatically: if the frontmost window cannot be resolved the helper fails and
says to ask for the display explicitly, rather than quietly archiving the whole
desktop.

EXPLAIN, CLARIFY and INTERPRET_SCREEN share one answering path in the assistant:
one lock, one streaming thread, one session log record, one terminal rendering.
The difference between them is a prompt builder and which model client is used,
so a fourth behaviour is a prompt plus a dispatcher entry. The three prompts
share one transcript framing helper in src/rvw/prompts.py.

The vision model is a second LocalLlm instance pointed at RVW_VLM_MODEL
(default identifier "meeting-vision") on the same OpenAI compatible endpoint,
so no new transport, and Phase 7 can move both at once. The image is inlined as
a base64 data URI because the endpoint takes no file paths.

At start-up the assistant now also says whether a vision model is loaded. An
absent vision model is reported as information, not as a failure: archiving
screenshots does not need one.

Neither of those two model clients trusts the endpoint to serve the model it was
asked for, because this LM Studio build does not; see bug 2 below for what it
does instead and what the client checks as a result.


Permissions
-----------
All of them are now checked and requested by one script, util/init_permissions.sh,
called from util/init.sh. It explains why each permission is needed, works out
which application macOS will attribute the grants to, and probes each permission
by performing the protected action with the real helper, because performing the
action is the only way to raise the system prompt. A refusal is reported with the
settings pane to open (-open opens it) and the tccutil command that makes macOS
ask again (-reset does it).

  Microphone        audio_capture --source mic
  Audio Recording   audio_capture --source system, the Core Audio process tap
  Screen Recording  screen_capture, ScreenCaptureKit
  Accessibility     Hammerspoon, which owns the hotkeys

Run on the M3 on 2026-08-16 from the IDE application (com.exafunction.windsurf):
microphone and system audio are granted, screen recording had been declined, and
Hammerspoon does not have Accessibility, so no hotkey works there yet.

The grants follow the launching application, so Terminal.app, iTerm, Emacs.app
and the IDE each prompt once; run the script from whichever one will launch
bin/rvw. Hammerspoon's Accessibility grant is its own and is needed only once,
because the hotkeys only send a command through a socket and the daemon spawns
the helpers. Starting the daemon from Hammerspoon or from a LaunchAgent would
move that responsibility; a LaunchAgent is the way to stop depending on the
launching terminal altogether, which belongs in Phase 6.


Verified on the M4 Air, 2026-08-21
----------------------------------
Everything below was exercised against the real helpers and the real model, with
speech played into the system audio tap by "say" so that the whole path from
sound to answer was the real one.

Permissions. util/init_permissions.sh now reports all four as granted:
microphone, audio recording, screen recording, and Hammerspoon's accessibility.

Screen capture. bin/screen_capture captured the frontmost window and printed the
metadata for it; three captures in one burst produced three PNGs with distinct
millisecond names and three complete sidecar JSON files, application, bundle id,
window title, window id, size, bytes and both timestamps included.

Recognition. Two spoken sentences became two transcript segments on the "them"
stream within about ten seconds of being spoken.

CLARIFY did the job it exists for. Whisper heard "The least timeout on the
coordinator" and "exponential back off"; the clarification returned "lease
timeout" and "backoff", marked both as repairs, and added two sentences of
meaning. This is the first evidence that the shorter window and the different
prompt earn their place beside EXPLAIN.

EXPLAIN took 78 s from hotkey to finished answer for a 60 s window with the
model already loaded, which confirms the Phase 1 finding rather than softening
it: the 35B model is not usable live on 32 GB. It explained six terms correctly
and repaired "right amplified" to "write amplified"; it also repaired "right
path" to "read path" where "write path" was what had been said. Repairs are
reported honestly but they are still guesses, and a wrong one reads exactly like
a right one.

Latency, then, remains the Air's problem, and it is a model sizing problem, not
a Phase 2 problem.


Two bugs this verification found
--------------------------------
1. Whichever audio probe ran second always reported its permission missing.
   Both audio probes read var/log/audio_capture.launcher.log, because the
   launcher names the log after the program and there is only one audio helper.
   The launcher outlives its helper by the moment it takes to report the exit
   and holds that log open for appending, so the previous launcher's "exited
   with status 0" landed in the log the next probe had just emptied, and that
   probe read it as its own helper's verdict. A probe now waits for the launcher
   to record the exit before returning, which leaves the log quiet before
   anybody empties it again. Covered by test/test_permission_probe.py, which
   sources the script for its functions; that is what the guard around main() is
   for, and the test truncates its copy at the bare "exit" that ends the script,
   because sourcing that exit would end the shell.

2. INTERPRET_SCREEN was answered by the text model and called it a success.
   Asked for the identifier "meeting-vision", which nothing serves, this LM
   Studio build neither refuses nor loads anything: it answers with whatever
   model is loaded and names that substitute in the response. The screenshot
   came back described in convincing detail by qwen3.6-35b-a3b, and nothing
   anywhere said that no vision model had seen it. Confirmed directly against
   the endpoint: a request naming "totally-nonexistent-model-xyz" was answered
   with "model": "meeting-assistant".

   The endpoint therefore cannot be trusted to serve what it was asked for, so
   src/rvw/llm.py now checks both ends. A client that does not load on demand
   refuses to ask anything of an identifier that is not being served, and every
   streamed chunk carries the answering model, so a substitution is caught even
   when the listing looked right. INTERPRET_SCREEN asks what is loaded before it
   promises an answer, and now replies

     OK screenshot saved as ....png; not interpreted: no model is loaded as
     'meeting-vision'

   which keeps the archival save, the part that matters, and stops passing one
   model's answer off as another's.


